1
00:00:00,238 --> 00:00:01,160
Welcome to Starry Hope.

2
00:00:02,670 --> 00:00:05,320
Today we're looking at what Omachi is, under the hood.

3
00:00:06,062 --> 00:00:10,140
You may have seen it in the video already, tiled windows and bright color themes.

4
00:00:10,702 --> 00:00:15,540
It looks like one finished product, but it's built from separate projects stacked on top of each other.

5
00:00:16,302 --> 00:00:21,800
We'll go up through those projects layer by layer, then see why it's built this way and what version 4 changed.

6
00:00:22,606 --> 00:00:26,840
After that come updates, the people and the money behind the project, and what critics say.

7
00:00:28,590 --> 00:00:31,240
Umachi comes from DHH, David Heinemeier Hanson.

8
00:00:31,726 --> 00:00:34,180
He sums it up at the start of his launch video for version 4.

9
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:44,420
Umachi is a beautiful, modern, and opinionated Linux system built on top of Arch, HyperLinux, and now Quickshell.

10
00:00:45,326 --> 00:00:48,500
Those are three separate projects, stacked one on another.

11
00:00:49,358 --> 00:00:50,760
At the bottom is Arch Linux.

12
00:00:51,662 --> 00:00:53,740
It's the base everything else is built on.

13
00:00:54,542 --> 00:00:58,360
Arch is popular with those who like to build and customize their systems by hand.

14
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:02,240
Omachi does all the hard customization work for you.

15
00:01:03,406 --> 00:01:12,240
Hyperland runs on top of Arch. It's a tiling compositor, the program that draws every window and arranges them side by side, with nothing overlapping.

16
00:01:13,134 --> 00:01:14,780
Omachi doesn't build its own copy.

17
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:19,500
Hyperland installs straight from Arch. On its own, Hyperland comes bare.

18
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:25,040
DHH wrote that you have to figure out everything yourself, even a lock screen or a menu bar.

19
00:01:26,060 --> 00:01:28,000
Quickshell supplies those missing parts.

20
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:32,140
It's a toolkit for building a desktop's bar, menus, and panels.

21
00:01:32,846 --> 00:01:34,280
And here, it draws all of them.

22
00:01:35,534 --> 00:01:37,800
The project's own code forms the next layer.

23
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:44,240
Omachi's base install is 147 packages, the separate pieces of software that make up the system.

24
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:47,480
127 come from Arch's own repositories.

25
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:51,500
The other 20 come from one extra repository that the project runs itself.

26
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:56,540
None come from the AUR, the Arch User Repository of Community Build Scripts.

27
00:01:57,262 --> 00:02:03,020
The project's own code ships as two ordinary Arch packages, called omachi and omachi-settings.

28
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:09,300
Their scripts are installed in the system's bin folder, where programs live, each named omachi-something.

29
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:13,120
4.0.4 has 443 of them.

30
00:02:13,838 --> 00:02:15,600
One omachi command routes to them.

31
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:18,900
omachi theme-set runs the theme-set script.

32
00:02:19,438 --> 00:02:22,680
That puts more than 400 scripts behind one name you can remember.

33
00:02:23,502 --> 00:02:25,960
Most of the other files live in one system folder.

34
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:29,420
Slash user, slash share, slash omachi.

35
00:02:30,638 --> 00:02:32,500
Below Arch is one more layer.

36
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:33,540
The Linux kernel.

37
00:02:34,190 --> 00:02:35,560
The core that talks to the hardware.

38
00:02:36,360 --> 00:02:41,740
Since 4.0.4, the project ships its own version of the Linux kernel, the default on most PCs.

39
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:47,060
The release notes say it's tuned to keep the desktop responsive, even under heavy load or memory pressure.

40
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:53,500
On a system upgraded from an older version, the previous kernel stays installed, in case the new one can't boot.

41
00:02:54,798 --> 00:02:56,280
Your own settings are the top layer.

42
00:02:56,974 --> 00:02:59,360
Hyperland's config file shows how the layers stack.

43
00:03:00,110 --> 00:03:03,320
The file loads the shipped defaults first, and your own files after them.

44
00:03:03,854 --> 00:03:08,320
A comment in that file says this lets package updates improve the defaults without rewriting your files.

45
00:03:09,198 --> 00:03:12,080
New defaults also don't reach your existing config files on their own.

46
00:03:12,846 --> 00:03:16,740
A resync command copies them in, but it resets your files to the defaults.

47
00:03:17,486 --> 00:03:20,540
A few system files under slash etc work the other way.

48
00:03:21,038 --> 00:03:23,460
An update overwrites them, and any edits you made are lost.

49
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:30,260
DHH has explained why he chose these layers, and who the project is for.

50
00:03:31,054 --> 00:03:35,980
The reason for Arch goes back to DHH's first attempt, Omacub, in June 2024.

51
00:03:36,782 --> 00:03:40,840
It was a script that turned a fresh Ubuntu install into a full web development setup.

52
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:44,200
Ubuntu lacked many of the tools he wanted though.

53
00:03:44,654 --> 00:03:48,940
He wrote that he had to package a million of them himself in sort of janky ways.

54
00:03:49,774 --> 00:03:56,360
On the project's website, he says the ideas he cared about, like tiling and a keyboard first desktop, kept fighting the platform.

55
00:03:57,180 --> 00:04:01,360
Talking with Misha Vandenberg, he compared Ubuntu's parts to toy building blocks.

56
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:08,360
It was just a Lego set dumped on the floor, and then an invitation to build your own Millennium Falcon.

57
00:04:09,582 --> 00:04:11,360
That's not typically what I want out of a computer.

58
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:18,220
But what I realized was, if I want the perfect computer, I have to start from the smallest Lego blocks.

59
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:21,960
And the blocks that Ubuntu gave me were not small enough.

60
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:24,480
They were Duplos, not Legos.

61
00:04:24,942 --> 00:04:33,920
DHH released Omachi in June 2025, and described it as an opinionated setup of Arch and Hyper His first big idea is tiling.

62
00:04:34,446 --> 00:04:37,800
Each new window takes a share of the screen and the keyboard moves you between them.

63
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:42,480
DHH calls tiling an acquired taste, like champagne or coffee.

64
00:04:43,054 --> 00:04:46,360
He told Network Chuck how tiling feels to someone used to a mouse.

65
00:04:46,862 --> 00:04:53,700
And it's a really alien way of using a computer if you're used to using a mouse and dragging little windows around.

66
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:57,020
But once you get the hang of it, it's like a superpower.

67
00:04:57,020 --> 00:05:02,260
It's like you strap a jetpack on your back and boom, here goes productivity in front of the keyboard.

68
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:05,760
DHH's second big idea is strong defaults.

69
00:05:06,286 --> 00:05:09,500
In his words, opinions are about defaults, not options.

70
00:05:10,190 --> 00:05:13,860
The default setup arrives ready, and the options are still there if you want them.

71
00:05:14,606 --> 00:05:20,860
Out of the box, that means the foot terminal, the Chromium browser, the NeoVim editor, and Hey for email links.

72
00:05:21,422 --> 00:05:26,520
In his launch video, he uses the Japanese word omakase, the meal where the chef picks every course.

73
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:28,580
Because this is your computer, right?

74
00:05:29,038 --> 00:05:37,180
I am preparing it omikase for you so that when you unwrap this thing, after you install it, it takes all about one minute on a modern fast machine.

75
00:05:37,838 --> 00:05:38,520
It's beautiful.

76
00:05:38,900 --> 00:05:39,420
It's gorgeous.

77
00:05:39,540 --> 00:05:40,080
It's all set up.

78
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:43,920
Everything is integrated and you have all my favorite background images.

79
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:48,036
But then you can tweak it exactly how you like it.

80
00:05:48,206 --> 00:05:56,940
Whether that is the style or as we'll see a little later on, the plugins, you can tweak Make it just how you like it and you can make it yours.

81
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:01,700
And then, I mean, if you make a mistake here, of course, you can just restore to default and then boom, you're back to Emachi.

82
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:03,260
But you should make it yours.

83
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:06,900
You should put in your logo there and make it yours.

84
00:06:07,380 --> 00:06:11,640
His third big idea, new in version 4, is AI coding agents.

85
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:16,020
Version 4 comes wired for them, but you pick which agent to use.

86
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:17,280
None is set by default.

87
00:06:17,966 --> 00:06:23,420
In the project's own words, agents launched from the menu run unattended, without stopping to ask first.

88
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:28,840
DHH sees Linux as a natural match for agents, as he told Misha Vandenberg.

89
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:31,660
Linux is the perfect fit for AI.

90
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:39,560
Everything is a CLI, everything is a config file, everything can be scripted, everything can be encoded, everything can be captured.

91
00:06:40,238 --> 00:06:43,920
And therefore, everything can also be debugged and evolved.

92
00:06:44,590 --> 00:06:49,440
As for the audience, DHH says the project wasn't built to win over existing Linux users.

93
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:52,440
Early in his launch video, he named who it's for.

94
00:06:52,860 --> 00:06:53,120
Nope.

95
00:06:53,420 --> 00:07:04,240
This is a mission to give people on Windows and Mac a different, better, more malleable alternative as their operating system.

96
00:07:05,262 --> 00:07:12,000
The largest release so far, version 4, came out on August 14, 2026, about 14 months after the first.

97
00:07:12,760 --> 00:07:16,080
DHH called it one of the greatest software releases of his career.

98
00:07:16,846 --> 00:07:21,520
DHH says version 4 was downloaded more than 300,000 times in its first month.

99
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:24,440
The biggest change was the desktop itself.

100
00:07:25,102 --> 00:07:30,880
Before, eight separate programs handled it, one each for jobs like the bar, the launcher, and the lock screen.

101
00:07:31,854 --> 00:07:35,760
Version 4 replaced all eight with a single quick shell process that keeps running.

102
00:07:37,102 --> 00:07:42,220
A week before the release, DHH told Network Chuck what Arch, Hyperland, and Quick Shell let him build.

103
00:07:42,220 --> 00:07:50,680
All the menus, all the panels, all the bars, all the icons, all the everything is in this thing called Quickshell.

104
00:07:50,840 --> 00:08:05,440
And the combination of those three things, Arch, Hyperland, and Quickshell, has allowed me to build Umachi into this perfect distillation of aesthetics, productivity, and everything I love about computers.

105
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:11,400
In 4.0.4, the shell built on QuickShell is nearly 42,000 lines of code.

106
00:08:12,180 --> 00:08:13,500
Version 3 had almost none of it.

107
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:18,760
Its only file in QML, the language QuickShell uses, was a small login screen theme.

108
00:08:19,438 --> 00:08:22,480
That makes nearly all of today's shell new in version 4.

109
00:08:23,246 --> 00:08:24,500
Every part of the shell is a plugin.

110
00:08:25,020 --> 00:08:26,520
And 23 come built in.

111
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:29,580
Anyone can add more from a link to the plugin's code.

112
00:08:30,350 --> 00:08:34,000
If you add one, have an AI coding agent read through its code first.

113
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:38,420
The project said over a thousand plugins were created in the week after the launch.

114
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:42,520
Version 4 also changed where the project lives on your disk.

115
00:08:43,054 --> 00:08:47,840
Up to version 3, it was a downloaded copy of the code, in a hidden folder in your home directory.

116
00:08:48,462 --> 00:08:53,960
Version 4 moved it into the two system packages from earlier, to keep your changes separate from the shipped files.

117
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:59,780
Since version 4, you install it only from an ISO, the disk image you write to a USB stick.

118
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:05,380
The release notes say installs got about 30% faster, and the ISO shrank by over a gigabyte.

119
00:09:07,790 --> 00:09:14,910
Once the system is installed, updates run through one command, omachi update, which needs at least 10 gigabytes of free space.

120
00:09:15,726 --> 00:09:19,030
It first saves a snapshot, a copy of the system as it is right then.

121
00:09:19,822 --> 00:09:25,110
After that, it updates the packages and runs migrations, small scripts that fix up existing installs.

122
00:09:25,670 --> 00:09:28,030
4.0.4 carries 106 of them.

123
00:09:28,030 --> 00:09:33,910
The usual Arch upgrade command, sudo pacman-syu, doesn't run here.

124
00:09:34,670 --> 00:09:37,970
A hook, a check that runs before the upgrade starts, blocks it.

125
00:09:38,530 --> 00:09:42,390
Its message starts with, whoa partner, and points you back to a machi update.

126
00:09:43,214 --> 00:09:45,970
One extra setting on the command gets past the hook when you need it.

127
00:09:46,770 --> 00:09:48,890
If an update goes wrong, there's a way back.

128
00:09:49,530 --> 00:09:54,230
Snapper, the snapshot tool, keeps the last five snapshots, all taken at update time.

129
00:09:54,862 --> 00:09:59,370
The boot menu, liminy, lists them, so you start the system as it was before the update.

130
00:10:00,078 --> 00:10:01,990
That rollback has been there since version 2.

131
00:10:02,770 --> 00:10:04,370
There are also four update channels.

132
00:10:04,770 --> 00:10:10,110
Stable, a release candidate channel, Edge, and Dev, which is meant for the project's own developers.

133
00:10:12,622 --> 00:10:16,080
The code has grown fast, and so has the group of people behind it.

134
00:10:16,814 --> 00:10:23,060
The code started on June 1, 2025, with one commit from DHH of 836 lines.

135
00:10:23,758 --> 00:10:28,560
By 4.0.4, the code had grown to about 140,000 lines.

136
00:10:29,060 --> 00:10:31,720
That's about 170 times the size of the first commit.

137
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:36,400
605 names appear in the development branch's history.

138
00:10:37,180 --> 00:10:42,620
But DHH still makes most of the commits – about 72% as of 4.0.4.

139
00:10:43,470 --> 00:10:46,180
Ryan Hughes is second, with about 12%.

140
00:10:47,420 --> 00:10:52,780
Nearly 800 commits on the development branch also name an AI model as a co-author, most Christoph and Claude.

141
00:10:53,262 --> 00:10:55,400
The first of those appeared in October 2025.

142
00:10:56,340 --> 00:11:00,420
DHH wrote that version 4 has almost exclusively been built by agents.

143
00:11:01,582 --> 00:11:05,720
Until version 4, DHH says it was mostly him and Ryan running everything.

144
00:11:06,350 --> 00:11:10,920
A core team formed in August 2026, including Ryan Hughes and Toby Lutka.

145
00:11:11,662 --> 00:11:15,640
Later, it added a kernel lead, a lead for the shell, and a head of security.

146
00:11:16,462 --> 00:11:21,700
The kernel's lead developer, Christoph Wilczynski, Money was the first full-time hire of the foundation behind the project.

147
00:11:22,478 --> 00:11:27,480
The Primogen, one of the hosts of the Stand Up podcast, joined in September to lead quality testing with agents.

148
00:11:28,206 --> 00:11:32,360
His harness boots the desktop and virtual machines and lets agents drive it the way a person would.

149
00:11:33,806 --> 00:11:38,580
The Omicom Foundation, a non-profit that launched on August 21st, handles the money.

150
00:11:38,980 --> 00:11:43,600
It holds the trademarks and pays for infrastructure and the open-source developers the project depends on.

151
00:11:44,270 --> 00:11:47,960
On the Stand Up, DHH named some of the people who pledged money at the launch.

152
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:57,380
I'm incorporating the Omicron Foundation with $8 million in pledged funding from just like a few people you might have heard of.

153
00:11:57,480 --> 00:11:58,720
Maybe you've heard of Michael Dell.

154
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:00,520
Maybe you've heard of Toby Lutka.

155
00:12:00,740 --> 00:12:09,080
Maybe you've heard of Patrick Coulson or Matthew Prince or Brendan Arieb or Jason Freed or Jack Dorsey.

156
00:12:09,550 --> 00:12:11,180
Pledges have grown since then.

157
00:12:11,726 --> 00:12:15,280
The foundation's page now lists about $23.7 million.

158
00:12:15,280 --> 00:12:15,720
dollars.

159
00:12:16,494 --> 00:12:20,900
That includes 1.5 million in Grok tokens from SpaceX's AI arm.

160
00:12:21,678 --> 00:12:23,640
Some of that money pays for the projects underneath.

161
00:12:24,462 --> 00:12:29,480
The foundation is the exclusive sponsor of Hyperland and its creator, Vaxri, for three years.

162
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,300
The project hasn't been without its critics, though.

163
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:40,660
Several critics on X called it only dot files, the hidden config files people keep for their own setups.

164
00:12:41,326 --> 00:12:48,480
One post called it an Arch installer plus a Hyperland Rice, which is a custom desktop look, plus DHH's favorite apps.

165
00:12:49,102 --> 00:12:55,360
Another, from Christian Lempa, called it Arch, a tiling compositor, and someone else's defaults with a famous name on the ISO.

166
00:12:56,334 --> 00:13:00,620
DHH has conceded that the .files label wasn't entirely wrong at the start.

167
00:13:01,326 --> 00:13:04,860
On the Tech Over Tea podcast, he described how that label fits his own setup today.

168
00:13:05,294 --> 00:13:06,900
I mean, founder's privilege here.

169
00:13:07,118 --> 00:13:09,900
When I'm up and running with Emachi, it's literally my system.

170
00:13:10,060 --> 00:13:11,460
I don't have .files anymore.

171
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:13,520
The .files is the distro.

172
00:13:13,980 --> 00:13:19,100
And I mean, I think that's a wonderful circle of joke here about how things started and now people say it's .files.

173
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:21,240
I'm like, actually, you're kind of right.

174
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:35,760
On Lex Friedman's podcast, DHH compared his goal with what first drew people to Arch. The people who were attracted to Arch in the first place were the ones who were attracted to building everything by hand themselves, doing it the hard way because they like to do it hard.

175
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:39,172
And I'm building the polar opposite of that."

176
00:13:39,502 --> 00:13:45,700
Another critic on X says version 4 moved the pretty parts into one process, but the glue is still fragile scripts.

177
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:50,940
In 4.0.4, 438 of the commands are bash scripts.

178
00:13:51,740 --> 00:13:57,580
Some posts on X call the project vibe-coded, meaning written by AI without enough care.

179
00:13:59,360 --> 00:14:03,780
Security drew criticism after the launch, and two defaults changed within weeks.

180
00:14:04,340 --> 00:14:08,720
4.0.1 was mostly security fixes, checked by a new security team.

181
00:14:09,454 --> 00:14:13,540
Up to 4.0.0, the installer put you in the Docker group.

182
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:16,640
Membership in that group amounts to full control of the system.

183
00:14:17,360 --> 00:14:19,460
4.0.1 made that opt-in.

184
00:14:20,174 --> 00:14:27,100
The project's package repository also accepted packages without a signature, the digital stamp that proves where a package came from.

185
00:14:27,740 --> 00:14:30,140
4.0.2 made signatures required.

186
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:36,980
Since then, the foundation has funded a $100,000 bug bounty, which pays people who report security holes.

187
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:42,400
A few interesting changes are in development for a future version.

188
00:14:43,220 --> 00:14:48,460
They include support for Macs with Apple's own chips and for laptops with Qualcomm Snapdragon chips.

189
00:14:49,280 --> 00:14:53,660
Others include a dock for floating windows and the desktop translated into 50 languages.

190
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:57,060
That's Omachi.

191
00:14:58,222 --> 00:15:01,860
Under the bright themes sits Arch Linux running on the project's own kernel.

192
00:15:02,740 --> 00:15:06,100
Hyperland arranges the windows and Quickshell draws the bar and menus.

193
00:15:06,926 --> 00:15:10,600
On top of that come hundreds of Omachi's own scripts and your own settings.

194
00:15:11,480 --> 00:15:17,300
Omachi started as one developer's personal setup, and it now has a foundation and a core team behind it.

195
00:15:18,062 --> 00:15:23,100
It's for people who want a finished Linux desktop without building it by hand, but still want to make it their own.

196
00:15:24,622 --> 00:15:30,740
If you'd like to try this yourself, every source we used is in the full guide on starryhope.com linked below.

197
00:15:31,340 --> 00:15:33,860
If this video helped, a like and a subscribe help the channel.

198
00:15:34,382 --> 00:15:34,940
Thanks for watching.

